WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.947
Missing information is a design choice, not something we should leave accidental.

00:00:04.947 --> 00:00:09.067
NULL represents unknown, missing, or not-applicable information.

00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:14.697
It is not the same as zero, an empty string, or false.

00:00:14.697 --> 00:00:17.902
For each column, ask whether missingness makes sense.

00:00:17.902 --> 00:00:23.477
A patient medical record number may be required, so it should not be nullable.

00:00:23.477 --> 00:00:30.002
Email may be optional. An appointment start time is required for a scheduled appointment.

00:00:30.002 --> 00:00:35.357
A cancellation time only makes sense when an appointment is cancelled, so it may be nullable.

00:00:35.357 --> 00:00:40.847
A data dictionary documents these decisions.

00:00:40.847 --> 00:00:45.737
It should describe the column meaning, type, nullability, units, keys, and important business rules.

00:00:45.737 --> 00:00:52.482
This documentation helps future analysts interpret the database correctly.

00:00:52.482 --> 00:00:57.952
Connect this idea to a university system separating students, courses, sections, and enrollments.

00:00:57.952 --> 00:01:03.272
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:01:03.272 --> 00:01:11.867
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:16.942
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:16.942 --> 00:01:22.560
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
